## Graph analytics phase B — iterative metrics on a serverless Lakeflow job

Context
-------
Phase A (above) lifted the 500k triple cap by pushing the aggregatable metrics
into the graph store as SQL, but PageRank, connected components and the
clustering coefficient stayed unavailable above the cap because each needs
repeated passes over the graph. Phase B computes those three on a serverless
Databricks job and reads the result back, leaving only betweenness and
closeness (all-pairs shortest paths) unavailable in engine-side modes.

Implementation style: **generated SQL**, chosen over the PySpark DataFrame API
so the algorithms are verifiable without a cluster. Every statement the job
executes is emitted as a string and driven through injected `execute` / `scalar`
callables; the tests run the exact same statements against SQLite and compare
against a NetworkX oracle. A DataFrame implementation could only have been
tested on a live workspace.

Changes
-------
1. `src/jobs/graph_analytics_job.py` (new) — the job. Module-level SQL builders
   with no pyspark import (so tests can use them), plus a `main()` that imports
   pyspark lazily. Emits `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS` + `CREATE TABLE AS` pairs rather
   than `CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE`: portable, and the write truncates the Spark
   lineage between iterations. PageRank is power iteration with exact rank-mass
   conservation (no dangling nodes, so no re-normalisation); components are
   min-label propagation that stops when no label changes and reports
   non-convergence rather than publishing a wrong count; triangles are counted
   via a degree-oriented acyclic edge list, which enumerates each triangle
   exactly once and bounds work at high-degree hubs. Both iterative algorithms
   alternate between `_a` / `_b` tables so no step reads and writes the same
   relation. `validate_identifier` rejects non-identifier table names, which are
   interpolated unquoted.
2. `resources/graph_analytics.job.yml` (new) — serverless `spark_python_task`
   with an `environments` block, `max_concurrent_runs: 1`, failure email, and
   per-run `job_parameters`. No Maven libraries: GraphFrames would be the
   obvious choice but cannot be installed on serverless compute, which is why
   the algorithms are hand-rolled.
3. `databricks.yml` — added the `include: resources/*.yml` block the bundle
   previously lacked, so the job resource is deployed. `sync.exclude` already
   permits `src/jobs/`.
4. `src/back/core/graph_analysis/LakeflowRunner.py` (new) — resolves the job by
   name (exact, then `[dev <user>] ` prefixed, since DAB renames in development
   mode), triggers `run_now`, and polls `get_run` mapping life-cycle state onto
   task-tracker progress. Injectable client factory and sleep.
5. `src/back/core/graph_analysis/JobMetrics.py` (new) — the third compute mode.
   `resolve_spark_source` decides whether a graph is reachable from Spark at
   all (Delta: yes; Lakebase `managed_synced`: the UC table upstream of the
   sync; Lakebase `app_managed` and Neo4j: refused with an actionable reason).
   `compute` reuses `PushdownMetrics` verbatim for the aggregates so the two
   modes cannot drift, runs the job, then overlays per-node PageRank/clustering,
   the component count and per-type `avg_clustering`. Read-back keeps the
   bounded-payload contract: only the union of top-N per metric.
6. `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/DigitalTwin.py` — `compute_graph_metrics`
   gained the `job` mode plus `settings` / `on_progress`; a job failure degrades
   to pushdown rather than losing the user the aggregates. Added
   `build_job_metrics` and `analytics_output_table` (one output table per domain
   version, sanitised to `[A-Za-z0-9_]`).
7. `src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py` — `/metrics/compute` prefers `job` over
   `pushdown` when enabled and the source resolves; `/dtwin/sync/stats` returns
   `analytics_job_available`.
8. `src/front/templates/partials/dtwin/_query_analytics.html` — **fixed a phase
   A bug**: `analyticsCompute` refused every oversized run unconditionally even
   though the button was enabled and the server would have accepted it, so the
   phase A pushdown path was unreachable from the UI. The backstop now only
   blocks when no engine-side path exists. Added the job-mode banner, the
   *(computed on Databricks)* provenance label, and a mode-aware explanation on
   the unavailable-metric charts.
9. `src/shared/config/settings.py` — five `analytics_job_*` settings; the
   feature is opt-in (`analytics_job_enabled` defaults to `false`) because it
   needs a deployed bundle and Spark-readable data.
10. `pyproject.toml` — added `sqlglot` as a dev dependency so the cross-dialect
    parse checks actually run instead of skipping.

