================================================================================
Job mode fell back silently because no job name could be resolved
================================================================================

Context
-------
User report, after the job was deployed and the Settings → Global toggle turned
on: "pagerank, betweeness, closeness and clsutering are still not calculated".

Cause, from the dev log:

    compute_graph_metrics: job mode failed (No graph-analytics job name is
    configured (ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_NAME)) — falling back to SQL pushdown

Job mode is gated on two things, not one. The toggle decides *whether* to use
it; the runner still needs the job's name to resolve it. `DigitalTwin` derives
the name from `ontobricks_app_name` when `ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_NAME` is
unset, but that reads `DATABRICKS_APP_NAME`, which the Apps platform injects and
a local dev shell does not. So locally the name resolved to `""`, the runner
refused, and the run fell back to engine-side aggregation — which does not
compute those four metrics. A deployed run would have worked; only local dev was
affected.

The reason this was hard to see is the UI: `analytics_job_available` checked the
toggle, pushdown support and Spark-readability, but *not* whether a job name
existed. So the banner promised the full metric set while every run quietly
delivered the reduced one. That is the same class of dishonesty fixed earlier
today in the node-detail table, one layer further up.

Changes
-------
1. .env — added `ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_NAME=ontobricks-07x-graph-analytics`
   (backup at .env.bak.before-analytics). Local dev only; the deployed app
   derives it. The undecorated name is correct: DAB deploys the job as
   "[dev <user>] <name>" and LakeflowRunner suffix-matches on "] <name>".

2. src/back/core/helpers/DatabricksHelpers.py — new
   `resolve_analytics_job_name(settings)`: explicit setting wins, else derive
   `<app>-graph-analytics`, else `""`. Exported from `back.core.helpers`.
   `""` is documented as meaningful — it is the one state where job mode is
   configured but cannot run.

3. src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py — both availability sites (the analytics
   compute path and `/dtwin/sync/stats`) now require a resolvable job name
   before reporting job mode as available, so the banner cannot promise metrics
   the run will omit.

4. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/DigitalTwin.py — dropped the inline derivation
   in favour of the shared resolver, so the API and the runner cannot disagree
   about what the job is called.

5. tests/units/core/test_helpers.py — `TestResolveAnalyticsJobName`, 7 tests:
   explicit wins, derivation from app name, empty when neither is available
   (the local-dev case), whitespace is not a name, trimming, missing attributes
   do not raise, and — the drift guard — that the derived name matches the
   bundle's `${var.app_name}-graph-analytics`, since a rename there would
   silently disable job mode on a fresh deploy.

Verification
------------
- `LakeflowRunner("ontobricks-07x-graph-analytics").resolve_job_id()` against the
  live workspace returns 822506438871868, i.e. the exact lookup that was failing
  now succeeds. An earlier attempt at this check failed with "Could not list
  Databricks jobs"; that was the harness, not the app — the script had not loaded
  .env, so the SDK fell back to CLI auth and found four profiles in
  ~/.databrickscfg matching the host (DEFAULT, ontobricks-dev, default, and the
  fe-vm-bcayla-demos added for the deploy) and refused to choose. With .env
  loaded the app uses PAT auth and the ambiguity does not arise.
- The running server's environment confirmed to carry the variable, and
