================================================================================
Analytics toggle silently reverted to "off" by any other section's Save
================================================================================

Context
-------
User report: the Settings → Global option "Compute large-graph metrics on
Databricks" "is not saved in the registry".

The storage layer was not at fault. Verified directly against the Lakebase
registry (`global_config` JSONB, force-loaded past the service cache) that
`analytics_job_enabled = True` was present, that `GlobalConfigService._save`
merges rather than replaces, and that the Lakebase store's `save_global_config`
re-reads uncached and writes the whole dict. The GET/POST endpoints round-trip
correctly, and replaying the full Save sequence — analytics toggle followed by
`/settings/graph-engine-config`, which is what runs after it — left the value
intact.

The bug was in the browser, and it was a silent clobber rather than a failure to
write. Save step 3c posted `analyticsJobInput.checked` *unconditionally*, and the
handler is bound to all four `.btn-save-settings` buttons (Databricks, Lakebase,
Neo4j, Global). The checkbox markup has no `checked` attribute, so its default is
unchecked, and `loadAnalyticsJobEnabled()` swallowed any read failure into a
`console.log` while leaving that default in place. So a hydration GET that failed
— or simply had not resolved yet on a slow load — turned the *next* Save
anywhere in Settings into a write of `false` over the admin's `true`, with a
"All settings saved" toast and nobody having touched the control. The user
enables it, a later unrelated save reverts it, and it reads as "never saved".

Reproduced in a real browser before the fix: with the stored value `true` and
only the hydration GET aborted, a Save in the *Databricks* section sent
`{"analytics_job_enabled": false}` and the stored value became `false`.

Changes — src/front/static/config/js/settings.js
-----------------------------------------------
1. New closure-scoped `analyticsJobHydrated` flag, declared beside
   `graphDbLoaded`.
2. `loadAnalyticsJobEnabled()` clears the flag and disables the checkbox on
   entry, sets the flag and re-enables only after the value has been applied
   (and after the `success` check, so a `success: false` body does not count as
   hydrated), keeps a `catch` so the un-awaited call at load cannot reject
   upward, and adds a `finally` that writes an explicit warning into
   `#analyticsJobEnabledSource` when the read did not land. Clearing on entry
   makes it re-entrant: a later failed refresh cannot leave a stale "hydrated".
3. Step 3c posts only when hydrated. When the input exists but was never
   hydrated it pushes an error instead, so the toast reports that the value was
   left unchanged rather than claiming success. It pushes rather than returns, so
   the rest of the Save still completes.

Deliberately *not* changed: the toggle still posts on both check and uncheck. An
over-tight guard here would make it one-way — an admin could enable the job but
never disable it against an env var that enables it — so that direction was
explicitly re-tested.

4. tests/units/settings/test_analytics_job_toggle.py — new
   `TestUnhydratedCheckboxIsNeverPersisted`, 10 tests: the flag exists, the save
   is gated on it, the old ungated `if (analyticsJobInput) {` is gone, a failed
   read is reported rather than saved, the flag is cleared per read and set only
   after the value is applied and after the success check, the checkbox is
   disabled until hydrated, the failure reaches the source note, and the `catch`
   survives.

Verification (real Chromium, fresh profile per run, patched asset confirmed served)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Happy path unchanged: no console errors, checkbox ends enabled and matching the
  server, normal source note, and the toggle persists in *both* directions
  (`true`→`false` and `false`→`true`, each surviving a reload).
- The reproduction is closed: hydration GET aborted, stored value `true`, Save
  clicked in the Databricks section without touching the checkbox →
  `POST /settings/save-analytics-job-enabled` does not fire at all, stored value
  remains `true`, toast reads "Some settings failed to save: Analytics job:
  current value could not be read, so it was left unchanged.", the box is
  disabled and the note carries the warning. The other six sibling POSTs still
  returned 200, so the guard does not abort the handler.
- Also covered: a `200` with `success: false`, which produces no console error at
  all — the disabled box and the note are the only signals, which is why both
  were added.
- Re-entrancy: after a failed hydration, a successful reload re-enables the box,
  restores the note, and saving works again.
- The Save button returned to "Save" in every scenario; no uncaught exceptions.
- Full suite: uv run pytest -q -m "not scenario" → 3716 passed, 275 skipped,
  5 deselected in 29.94s
- Stored value left at `analytics_job_enabled: true` (the user's intended state),
  confirmed by a final GET.

Security note (pre-existing, not addressed)
-------------------------------------------
`GET /settings/graph-engine-config` returns the Neo4j password in plaintext, and
every Save from any section round-trips that full blob back in the request body.
Any authenticated user who can reach the settings endpoints can read the
credential, and it lands in browser devtools and any request logging. Worth
treating as a finding in its own right — it is unrelated to this bug and was left
alone.

