================================================================================
Name the reason large-graph job mode is unavailable, instead of telling an
admin to enable a setting they already enabled
================================================================================

Context
-------
With "Compute large-graph metrics on Databricks" ticked and stored
(analytics_job_enabled=true, source=admin), the Analytics page still showed the
amber banner ending with "...or enable Compute large-graph metrics on Databricks
in Settings -> Global (admin only)". That advice is useless to someone who has
already done it, and it gave no way to tell whether the toggle had failed, the
job name was missing, or the graph was unreadable from Spark.

Job mode needs four things: the admin toggle, an engine that can aggregate
server-side, a resolvable job name, and a graph Spark can read as a UC table.
resolve_spark_source() already returns a human-readable reason for its own
refusals - its docstring says the text is written for the person reading it in
the UI - but the stats endpoint discarded it with `[0]`, so every failure
collapsed into a bare False and the diagnosis lived only in the logs.

Investigation of the reported instance found no misconfiguration at all:
analytics_job_available is true and the blocked reason is empty for
`bigcustomers` (7,641,879 triples, Lakehouse/Delta engine, where the graph
already is a UC table). What the user was reading was a *stale analysis result*
rendered below the banner - "Last computed 1h ago (engine-side aggregation)"
with "Not computed for this graph." on PageRank/betweenness/closeness/clustering
- produced by a run made before the toggle was enabled. The live banner is now
the blue "will run on Databricks" one. Re-running the analysis is the fix for
that; the changes below make the same question answerable next time without a
log dive.

Changes
-------
1. src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py
   - New `_analytics_job_status(domain, settings, store, graph_name, *,
     pushdown_ok) -> (available, reason)` helper: checks the four prerequisites
     in order and returns the specific reason the first missing one failed,
     passing resolve_spark_source's own wording through verbatim. Returns an
     empty reason when the toggle is off, because then nothing is broken.
     `pushdown_ok` is a parameter rather than derived, because the two callers
     legitimately disagree: the analytics run also honours
     settings.analytics_pushdown_enabled, while the stats endpoint reports raw
     engine capability. Short-circuits before resolve_spark_source when the
     toggle is off, avoiding a needless remote synced-table lookup.
   - /dtwin/sync/stats now returns `analytics_job_blocked_reason` alongside
     `analytics_job_available`, populated only when an admin enabled the toggle
     and it still cannot run.
   - Replaced the duplicated four-condition expression at the analytics-run
     site with the shared helper.
   - The stats cache staleness guard now also requires
     `analytics_job_blocked_reason` to be present. It previously only inspected
     top_predicates[0].kind, so a payload predating any new field was served
     until expiry - which would have masked a setting the user had just changed.
     The refresh log line now reports which check failed.

2. src/front/templates/partials/dtwin/_query_analytics.html
   - New `_jobBlockedReason`, hydrated from the stats payload.
   - The amber over-limit banner now ends with "Compute large-graph metrics on
     Databricks is enabled, but the job cannot run: <reason>" when a reason is
     present, and only falls back to the "or enable it in Settings -> Global"
     advice when the toggle is genuinely off.

3. tests/units/dtwin/test_analytics_job_status.py (new, 19 tests)
   - Behavioural coverage of the helper: all prerequisites met; toggle off is
     unavailable *without* a reason and probes nothing else; each cause named
     (engine, job name mentions ONTOBRICKS_ANALYTICS_JOB_NAME, spark reason
     passed through verbatim); a reasonless refusal still explains itself;
     cause ordering.
   - Wiring checks that the reason reaches the banner, that the "go and enable
     it" advice sits only on the not-blocked branch, and that the stats cache
     treats a payload without the field as stale.

Also noted, not fixed (unrelated, pre-existing)
-----------------------------------------------
